Deliverable 3.1b: A timestamped performance_report_*.txt file containing all eight report sections listed above. Expected result: The generated file exists in your working directory (ls performance_report_*.txt shows it) and printing it to the terminal shows populated, non-empty values in every section.

Subtask 3.2: Advanced Logging and Trend Analysis
Step 1: Run a short advanced monitoring session that logs CPU, memory, and process snapshots with alert thresholds:

cat > advanced_monitor.sh << 'EOF'
#!/bin/bash

LOG_DIR="monitoring_logs"
mkdir -p "$LOG_DIR"

MONITOR_DURATION=60   # 1 minute for lab purposes
SAMPLE_INTERVAL=10    # 10 seconds
ALERT_CPU_THRESHOLD=80
ALERT_MEM_THRESHOLD=85

CPU_LOG="$LOG_DIR/cpu_$(date +%Y%m%d_%H%M%S).log"
MEM_LOG="$LOG_DIR/memory_$(date +%Y%m%d_%H%M%S).log"
PROC_LOG="$LOG_DIR/processes_$(date +%Y%m%d_%H%M%S).log"
ALERT_LOG="$LOG_DIR/alerts_$(date +%Y%m%d_%H%M%S).log"

touch "$ALERT_LOG"

log_cpu_metrics() {
    local timestamp
    timestamp=$(date '+%Y-%m-%d %H:%M:%S')
    local cpu_idle
    cpu_idle=$(top -bn1 | grep "Cpu(s)" | awk -F',' '{print $4}' | awk '{print $1}')
    local cpu_usage
    cpu_usage=$(echo "100 - $cpu_idle" | bc)
    local load_avg
    load_avg=$(uptime | awk -F'load average:' '{print $2}' | awk '{print $1}' | sed 's/,//')

    echo "$timestamp,$cpu_usage,$load_avg" >> "$CPU_LOG"

    if (( $(echo "$cpu_usage > $ALERT_CPU_THRESHOLD" | bc -l) )); then
        echo "$timestamp,CPU,High CPU usage: $cpu_usage%" >> "$ALERT_LOG"
    fi
}

log_memory_metrics() {
    local timestamp
    timestamp=$(date '+%Y-%m-%d %H:%M:%S')
    local mem_info
    mem_info=$(free -m | awk 'NR==2{printf "%d,%d,%d,%.1f", $2,$3,$4,$3*100/$2}')

    echo "$timestamp,$mem_info" >> "$MEM_LOG"

    local mem_percent
    mem_percent=$(echo "$mem_info" | cut -d',' -f4)
    if (( $(echo "$mem_percent > $ALERT_MEM_THRESHOLD" | bc -l) )); then
        echo "$timestamp,MEMORY,High memory usage: $mem_percent%" >> "$ALERT_LOG"
    fi
}

log_top_processes() {
    local timestamp
    timestamp=$(date '+%Y-%m-%d %H:%M:%S')
    echo "=== $timestamp ===" >> "$PROC_LOG"
    ps aux --sort=-%cpu | head -6 >> "$PROC_LOG"
    echo "" >> "$PROC_LOG"
}

echo "Starting advanced monitoring..."
echo "Duration: $MONITOR_DURATION seconds"
echo "Interval: $SAMPLE_INTERVAL seconds"
echo "Logs will be saved in: $LOG_DIR"

echo "Timestamp,CPU_Usage_Percent,Load_Average" > "$CPU_LOG"
echo "Timestamp,Total_MB,Used_MB,Free_MB,Usage_Percent" > "$MEM_LOG"
echo "Advanced Process Monitoring Log" > "$PROC_LOG"

for ((i=1; i<=MONITOR_DURATION/SAMPLE_INTERVAL; i++)); do
    log_cpu_metrics
    log_memory_metrics
    log_top_processes
    echo "Sample $i/$((MONITOR_DURATION/SAMPLE_INTERVAL)) completed"
    sleep $SAMPLE_INTERVAL
done

echo "Monitoring completed. Check logs in $LOG_DIR"
EOF

chmod +x advanced_monitor.sh
./advanced_monitor.sh
Step 2: Analyze the CPU trend data collected above:

cat > trend_analyzer.sh << 'EOF'
#!/bin/bash

# Performance trend analysis script
LOG_DIR="monitoring_logs"

analyze_cpu_trends() {
    echo "=== CPU TREND ANALYSIS ==="

    local cpu_log
    cpu_log=$(ls -t "$LOG_DIR"/cpu_*.log 2>/dev/null | head -1)

    if [ -z "$cpu_log" ] || [ ! -f "$cpu_log" ]; then
        echo "No CPU log files found in $LOG_DIR. Run advanced_monitor.sh first."
        return 1
    fi

    echo "Analyzing CPU data from: $cpu_log"

    local avg_cpu max_cpu min_cpu
    avg_cpu=$(awk -F',' 'NR>1{sum+=$2; count++} END{if(count>0) printf "%.2f", sum/count; else print "0"}' "$cpu_log")
    max_cpu=$(awk -F',' 'NR>1{if($2>max || NR==2) max=$2} END{print max}' "$cpu_log")
    min_cpu=$(awk -F',' 'NR>1{if($2<min || NR==2) min=$2} END{print min}' "$cpu_log")

    echo "Average CPU Usage: $avg_cpu%"
    echo "Maximum CPU Usage: $max_cpu%"
    echo "Minimum CPU Usage: $min_cpu%"

    echo ""
    echo "Identify peak CPU sample (timestamp and value):"
    awk -F',' 'NR>1{if($2>max || NR==2){max=$2; ts=$1}} END{print ts", "max"%"}' "$cpu_log"
}

analyze_memory_trends() {
    echo ""
    echo "=== MEMORY TREND ANALYSIS ==="

    local mem_log
    mem_log=$(ls -t "$LOG_DIR"/memory_*.log 2>/dev/null | head -1)

    if [ -z "$mem_log" ] || [ ! -f "$mem_log" ]; then
        echo "No memory log files found in $LOG_DIR. Run advanced_monitor.sh first."
        return 1
    fi

    echo "Analyzing memory data from: $mem_log"

    local avg_mem max_mem
    avg_mem=$(awk -F',' 'NR>1{sum+=$5; count++} END{if(count>0) printf "%.2f", sum/count; else print "0"}' "$mem_log")
    max_mem=$(awk -F',' 'NR>1{if($5>max || NR==2) max=$5} END{print max}' "$mem_log")

    echo "Average Memory Usage: $avg_mem%"
    echo "Peak Memory Usage: $max_mem%"
}

check_alerts() {
    echo ""
    echo "=== ALERT SUMMARY ==="

    local alert_log
    alert_log=$(ls -t "$LOG_DIR"/alerts_*.log 2>/dev/null | head -1)

    if [ -z "$alert_log" ] || [ ! -s "$alert_log" ]; then
        echo "No alerts were triggered during the monitoring window."
    else
        echo "Alerts found in $alert_log:"
        cat "$alert_log"
    fi
}

# Main execution
analyze_cpu_trends
analyze_memory_trends
check_alerts

echo ""
echo "Trend analysis completed."
EOF

chmod +x trend_analyzer.sh
./trend_analyzer.sh
Deliverable 3.2: The populated monitoring_logs/ directory containing CPU, memory, process, and alert log files, plus the terminal output of trend_analyzer.sh showing computed average, maximum, and minimum CPU usage, average and peak memory usage, and an alert summary. Expected result: trend_analyzer.sh runs to completion without syntax errors, printing numeric average/max/min CPU statistics, a peak-usage timestamp, memory statistics, and either an alert list or the "no alerts" message.

Expected Outcomes
A working monitoring toolkit (baseline, stress-test, reporting, and trend-analysis scripts) capable of capturing and summarizing real-time CPU and memory behavior on a Linux system
Demonstrated ability to correlate GUI observations in gnome-system-monitor with command-line data (ps, top, free) for the same workload
A complete, timestamped performance report and a trend analysis summary that a system administrator could use to justify a resource or configuration change
Conclusion
In this lab, students installed and configured gnome-system-monitor alongside complementary command-line tools to observe, stress-test, and analyze CPU and memory performance on a Linux system. Through hands-on tasks, students built a baseline monitoring script, generated controlled CPU and memory load with stress-ng and a bounded Python memory-leak simulation, and correlated GUI graphs with process-level data using ps and top. Students then practiced process priority management with renice, produced a comprehensive timestamped performance report, and built an advanced logging and trend-analysis pipeline that computed average, minimum, and peak resource usage while flagging threshold-based alerts. Together these exercises reinforced the core objectives of the lab: recognizing performance bottlenecks, distinguishing normal from abnormal resource consumption, and translating raw monitoring data into actionable optimization decisions. For further study, students are encouraged to extend the alerting logic to send notifications (for example, via email or a messaging webhook), integrate the logging scripts with a persistent time-series tool such as Prometheus and Grafana, and practice these same techniques against real multi-process workloads such as a web server or database under load.
